6. WHAT THE SOFTWARE DOES WITH YOUR DATA

Nothing leaves your Mac by way of Cupertino. The app reads your local Mail,
Notes and Reminders data on behalf of the MCP clients you connect to it, over a
socket that is a file on your own disk, restricted to your own user account.

Two honest limits on that sentence:

  (a) The MCP client you connect — Claude, an editor, whatever it is — is a
      separate program with its own network behaviour and its own privacy terms.
      Cupertino hands it data. What that program then does is between you and it.

  (b) Some operations ask Mail.app to send a message or check for new mail.
      Mail.app then does network I/O, as it would if you had clicked the button
      yourself. Cupertino opens no socket; it is not true that nothing at all
      leaves the machine.

7. FULL DISK ACCESS

Cupertino asks for Full Disk Access, and that is the point of it: one grant to
one notarized binary from an identifiable developer, instead of the same grant
handed separately to every editor that might spawn a server.

You may withdraw the grant at any time in System Settings. The app degrades and
reports what it cannot reach; it does not work around the refusal.

